                <h2>Protect Your Skin Health</h2>
                <p>Skin cancer is the most common form of cancer in the United States, affecting 1 in 5 Americans by age 70. The good news? When caught early, skin cancer is highly treatable. That's why regular skin cancer screenings are so important.</p>
                
                <h3>What is a Skin Cancer Screening?</h3>
                <p>A skin cancer screening is a visual examination of your skin by a dermatology professional. During the exam, we systematically check your entire body for any suspicious moles, spots, or lesions that could indicate skin cancer or pre-cancer.</p>
                
                <h3>What We Look For</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Basal Cell Carcinoma</strong> – The most common type, often appearing as a pearly bump</li>
                    <li><strong>Squamous Cell Carcinoma</strong> – Often appears as a scaly red patch or nodule</li>
                    <li><strong>Melanoma</strong> – The most serious form, often appearing as an unusual mole</li>
                    <li><strong>Actinic Keratoses</strong> – Pre-cancerous rough, scaly patches</li>
                    <li><strong>Atypical Moles</strong> – Moles that may have a higher risk of becoming melanoma</li>
                </ul>
                
                <h3>The Screening Process</h3>
                <p>Your skin cancer screening will include:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Review of your personal and family skin cancer history</li>
                    <li>Full-body visual examination of your skin</li>
                    <li>Dermoscopy (magnified examination) of any concerning spots</li>
                    <li>Documentation of any lesions requiring monitoring</li>
                    <li>Biopsy of suspicious lesions if needed</li>
                    <li>Discussion of your risk factors and prevention strategies</li>
                </ul>
                
                <h3>Who Should Get Screened?</h3>
                <p>We recommend annual skin cancer screenings for:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Adults with a personal history of skin cancer</li>
                    <li>Those with a family history of melanoma</li>
                    <li>People with many moles (more than 50)</li>
                    <li>Fair-skinned individuals who burn easily</li>
                    <li>Anyone with a history of blistering sunburns</li>
                    <li>People who have used tanning beds</li>
                    <li>Those with weakened immune systems</li>
                    <li>Anyone with a new or changing mole</li>
                </ul>
                
                <h3>Prevention Tips</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Apply broad-spectrum SPF 30+ sunscreen daily</li>
                    <li>Wear protective clothing and hats outdoors</li>
                    <li>Seek shade during peak sun hours (10am-4pm)</li>
                    <li>Never use tanning beds</li>
                    <li>Perform monthly self-skin exams at home</li>
                </ul>
